			<div class="content" id="post-content-4820713">
									

<p>Hey there Hinroe, I'll be critting your idea today!<br />
Note that I am neither a particularly experienced nor official reviewer, so take what I say with a grain of salt. With that being said, let's begin the crit!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Open&nbsp;Section-by-Section&nbsp;+</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close&nbsp;Section-by-Section&nbsp;-</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><span style="font-size:large;">Elevator Pitch</span></p>
<p>Ok, so I really like this as a concept (even if I haven't had a great history with cats, I'm more of a dog person). It's quite interesting and even ties in with some in-universe Foundation applications, like, as you said, for Task Forces such as Mu-13 or others that deal with extra-dimensional/paranormal entities. I love the idea of hardened MTF veterans with a bunch of cats as their watchdogs&#8230; or should that be watchcats? Seal of approval for this pitch from me.</p>
<p><span style="font-size:large;">Central Narrative</span></p>
<p>The narrative here is a bit iffy in my opinion due to two things: 1) the cat isn't being protected by anything (as evidenced by the 'lured back with catnip part'), and 2) the D-class is ripped to shreds. The first is just a bit reckless and probably wouldn't get Ethics Committee approval (pitting a cat against an threat level yellow extraphysical entity). The second is how the D-class got shredded by the interaction between the cat and the entity. I don't understand how the cat could shred the D-class, so this needs some more explanation. Other than that though, it's pretty good.</p>
<p><span style="font-size:large;">Hook/Attention Grabber</span></p>
<p>Had me in four words. Brilliant. Creates enough curiosity to draw the reader in without giving away all of the information ('Cats seeing ghosts? How tho?'), and is the perfect lead-in to the containment procedures and description.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;General&nbsp;Thoughts&nbsp;+</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close&nbsp;General&nbsp;Thoughts&nbsp;-</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>To summarise, I enjoy the concept behind this idea and I think it has a lot of potential, both narratively and in-Foundation-universe. However, your narrative may need a bit more work. Consider having an incident involving a regular housecat and a civilian? That's my input on that, just brainstorm some ideas while you reply.</p>
<p>And to summarise your idea in one sentence:<br />
Cute cats assist soldiers in seeing ghosts (and it's really cool).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Anyway, that's all for my crit today. Thanks for reading, and I hope this helps!</p>

